eo-Ä-ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ-=ğ elIII.ep Mouse clix eo ğ=ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ-Ä--

        You can select a tiles by clicking on it with the left mouse button.
Keeping this button pulled down will allow you to move the map without using
the keyboard.
        The center button (if you have one) used in the left side will
temporaly select a tile, as if you were using the [T] key.
        The right button, used at left, will copy a tile to the pointed tile,
in a way it behaves like the space key.

eo-Ä-ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ-=ğ elIV.ep Setting up a tunneleo ğ=ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ-Ä--

Copy your two tiles that are the entrances of your tunnel. Move the
box-pointer on one, and select it if the [T] key (or clic on it with the
middle button). Once you will move the pointer, this tile will appear in
a dashed box. Move on the second tunnel and clic on the [GO TO] box that
is on the right of your screen. Now you have set up the first way: for
now if your were playing on this level you could go from this place to
the dashed one, but from the dashed to this one. So you also have to set
up the tunnel in the other way: select the current tiles with [T], move
on the former and clic on [GO TO]. That's all.

Notice that with this technique you can also typeset some kind of
triangular tunnel. Let A, B, C be the entrances, you could setup the way
like that A->B, B->C, and C->A.

(0x0) for the coordinate of the destination denote a non-setup way. That's
why you can't put a tunnel on the (0x0) position.

eo-Ä-ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ-=ğ elV.ep Setting up a spriteeo ğ=ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ-Ä--

With some set of tiles (like the Egyptian one), there are tiles (like
palm trees) designed to be used as "sprites", i.e. put over others with
transparancy. You have to use the [S] key for this:
1)  copy the ground tile with [space] or the first button
2)  copy the sprite tile with [S]
(if you don't see any sprite on the level, press F3)

eo-Ä-ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ-=ğ elVI.ep Setting up a animationeo ğ=ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ-Ä--

You may have noticed that during the game, some tiles are animated:
color cycle (like the speed arrows) or moving objects (ventilators).
Each frame of an animation is actually a separate tile. The different
tiles of an animations are drawn horizontaly in the sets. To setup
an animation, you just have to select the first one and copy it into
your level. Optionnaly, press [Ctrl+Return] to see the animation.
When the orange box is on an animated tile, you should find a button
on the right to modify the speed: the greater the number is, the slower
the animation goes.

eo-Ä-ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ-=ğ elVII.ep Setting up departureseo ğ=ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ-Ä--

Select a tile and press the [D] key. Then look at the right side, select a
color and clic on the arrow you want. (if you don't see any vehicle on the
level, press F3)

eo-Ä-ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ-=ğ elVIII.ep Saving eo ğ=ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ-Ä--

Saving is done automaticaly when you quit with [Esc]. The level is saved
under a subdirectory EXTRALVL\ with the name you choose and the .LVL
suffixe. Heroes does not provide a way to rename a level, but it is safe
to do it manualy with DOS' ren or in any Windows file manager provinding
that you use only these characters : A-Z and 0-9. (actualy the problem
is that using a character unavailable in the Heroes font may crash the game).
